---
import InnerPage from "../layouts/InnerPage.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import CompareTable from "../components/inner/CompareTable.astro";
import { VS_INDEX } from "../data/vs-index.mjs";
import { latestRelease, releaseLine, fillRelease } from "../lib/release";

/* Sourced from docs/COMPARISON.md, which is itself sourced from a survey of
 * Restreamer's issue trackers.
 *
 * Every row here is a capability polyemesis has and the others do not, stated
 * as a fact a reader can check against their own install rather than as a
 * claim. docs/COMPARISON.md carries the full picture including where the other
 * tools lead, for anyone who wants it. */

/* [capability, polyemesis, Restreamer, restream.io]
 *
 * polyemesis is a column in the data rather than a hardcoded "Yes" in the
 * markup. It was the latter for one commit, which works exactly until somebody
 * adds a row this project does not win — and then the table asserts something
 * false while looking maintained. */
/* [row, polyemesis, Restreamer, restream.io]
 *
 * The shared ground, from docs/COMPARISON.md's "Where the three overlap".
 * A page that lists only differences is read as cherry-picked even when every
 * row of it is true — and it is genuinely useful to a reader to know that all
 * three do the basic job, because it tells them the decision is about the
 * audio and nothing else. Rows where polyemesis is NOT ahead are kept as they
 * are; that is the point of the section. */
const shared = [
  ["Ingest once, fan out to several platforms", "Yes", "Yes", "Yes"],
  // "not assessed" on the competitor columns, and this is the gap the amended
  // sourcing rule was caught leaving open. Both rows asserted "Yes" for both
  // products on the strength of the page's general re-check footer. A footer
  // saying the page was surveyed is not a citable source for a specific cell:
  // it says somebody looked at the products, not that anybody checked THIS.
  // Neither row has a line in the facts file, and neither has a quoted primary
  // source beside it, so neither may assert.
  ["Reconnects when a platform drops", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  ["Live health and status you can watch", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  // Restreamer's cell said "Yes" until 2026-08-09 and was wrong: recording is
  // an open feature request on their own tracker (datarhei/restreamer#692,
  // opened 2024-02-14, still open when checked). "No built-in" rather than a
  // bare "No" because datarhei Core underneath it can write files — the
  // Restreamer product simply does not put recording in front of you, which is
  // why the request exists. See docs/COMPARISON.md for the full footnote.
  ["Recording", "Multitrack, stream-copied", "No built-in", "not assessed"],
  // Restreamer's cell said "REST" and implied Prometheus was ours alone. Its
  // own README advertises resource monitoring "optionally by Prom-Metrics" and
  // datarhei Core documents Prometheus support, and it serves GraphQL as well
  // as REST. Neither half of the old cell survived contact with their docs, so
  // the row states what we do and does not characterise theirs.
  //
  // "not assessed" RATHER THAN BLANK, and the difference is the whole point. An
  // empty cell in a table whose other cells say "Yes" and "n/a" does not read as
  // "we did not look" -- it reads as "no", which is the false implication the
  // comment above was written to avoid and which the blank quietly reintroduced.
  // Saying nothing is only honest if the reader can tell that is what happened.
  ["Metrics / API", "Prometheus + REST", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  // All five are offered and all five are probed with a real one-frame encode
  // at startup. What is EXPERIMENTAL is narrower and does not belong in a table
  // cell: the per-encoder FLAGS handed to NVENC, QSV, VA-API and AMF were read
  // out of FFmpeg's option tables rather than measured on silicon. VideoToolbox
  // is not in that set. The footnote under this table carries it, and
  // docs/COMPARISON.md carries the same footnote — change both together.
  ["Hardware encoding", "NVENC, QSV, VA-API, VideoToolbox, AMF", "Yes", "n/a"],
  ["Unified chat", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  ["Self-hosted", "Yes", "Yes", "No"],
  // "No configured cap", not "Unlimited". There is no limit in the software,
  // but upload bandwidth and CPU are real: measured at roughly 4% of one core
  // per destination, a 4-core box runs out somewhere near 96. Claiming
  // "unlimited" invites the one support thread nobody can answer.
  ["Simultaneous destinations", "No configured cap", "not assessed", "2–5 by plan (checked 15 Aug 2026)"],
  ["Cost", "Your hardware", "Your hardware", "Subscription"],
];

const wins = [
  ["Per-destination audio mix from one multitrack ingest", "Yes", "No", "No"],
  ["Channel-level mix matrix with per-cell gain", "Yes", "No", "No"],
  // "No" against Restreamer was wrong as written: it mounts a filter select per
  // publication service and loudnorm is one of the filters offered, so it DOES
  // apply a per-destination loudness filter. What it does not offer is a
  // configurable target -- its control is an on/off checkbox emitting the bare
  // string `loudnorm`, taking FFmpeg's defaults for I, LRA and TP. That is the
  // real difference. The two cells were blank, and a blank in a table that shows
  // a glyph per cell reads as "no" without saying so; they now carry the
  // document's own words (docs/COMPARISON.md: Restreamer "Filter only", with
  // footnote 4 above, restream.io "No").
  ["Per-destination loudness TARGET you set — I, LRA and TP, measured after routing", "Yes", "Filter only", "No"],
  // "not assessed" ON FOUR ROWS BELOW, and it is not timidity.
  //
  // The copy rule this project holds itself to is explicit: if we cannot source
  // them, the row states only what polyemesis does and leaves the others blank
  // rather than asserting a No we have not checked. These four -- archive,
  // stems, annotations, typed SRT
  // rejections -- have no line in competitor-facts-2026-08-15.md for either
  // product. They were asserted "No" from somebody's impression.
  //
  // NOT LEFT BLANK, though, which is what the rule literally says. A blank cell
  // in a table whose neighbours read "Yes" and "n/a" is read as "No" by every
  // visitor; the honesty of saying nothing is lost if nobody can tell you said
  // nothing. Spelling it out is the same intent with the implication removed.
  //
  // The two mix rows ABOVE keep their "No": those trace to facts §1.A
  // (Restreamer cannot sum tracks) and §2.A (restream.io duplicates one stereo
  // track everywhere), which is exactly the sourcing these four lack.
  ["Multitrack archive — every ingest track preserved, stream-copied", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  ["Per-track stems as 24-bit WAV or FLAC, segment-aligned", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  ["Track annotations — what each incoming track actually is", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  ["Typed SRT rejections — the publisher is told why it was refused", "Yes", "not assessed", "not assessed"],
  // "Yes" on its own was the wrong shape for this cell, and wiring the feature
  // is what made it matter: Twitch refuses Enhanced Broadcasting without a
  // supported GPU, so on the headless VPS this audience actually runs, ticking
  // the toggle gets a quiet fallback to the ordinary ingest. A bare "Yes" in a
  // comparison table is the worst place to omit that. The copy rule is to state
  // it in the same block that announces the feature, not in a footnote.
  ["A second audio mix to the same destination, from one ingest", "Yes: Twitch Enhanced Broadcasting, needs a GPU", "—", "—"],
  ["Runs on hardware you control, no per-destination pricing", "Yes", "Yes", "No"],
];

/* THE LOSING ROWS, and they are the reason this page can be believed at all.
 *
 * docs/COMPARISON.md carries four "what X has that polyemesis does not" tables
 * and the site carried none of them, which left /comparison as the half of that
 * document that flatters the product. A comparison page that only wins is read
 * as marketing even when every row of it is true — and this one has the unusual
 * advantage of a source document that already did the honest work.
 *
 * Every line below is the same claim as its row in docs/COMPARISON.md, reworded
 * only where a table cell had to become a sentence. Nothing is softened: the
 * maturity row, the "you run a server or there is nothing to run" row and the
 * teams-and-roles row are the three most likely to lose a reader, and they are
 * the three that most need to be here rather than discovered after an evening.
 *
 * NOT a fifth `.cmp` table. Four two-column tables of prose is four horizontal
 * scrollers on a phone for content that is really a list of concessions, and the
 * `capability`-headed parity check in scripts/check-build.mjs is scoped to the
 * win table by design — a second table shape here would either trip it or
 * quietly opt out of it. Cards carry the same rows without either problem.
 *
 * @type {{who: string, what: string, rows: [string, string][], tail?: string}[]} */
const release = latestRelease();
const losses = [
  {
    who: "the OBS plugins",
    what: "obs-multi-rtmp and Aitum Multistream: free, GPL-2.0, installed in two minutes and already on the machine.",
    rows: [
      ["No server at all", "A plugin in OBS: no host to provision, no port to open. Here you run a server, or there is nothing to run."],
      ["Nothing new to learn", "The destinations sit in OBS beside the ones already there. Here it is a second UI and a second set of concepts."],
      ["No extra hop", "OBS talks to the platform directly, so nothing in the middle can fail. Here the server is one more thing between you and air."],
      ["Per-target video encoder settings, decided in OBS", "Here that is a shared rendition, decided on the server."],
    ],
    tail: "What they cost you is upload: N destinations is N uploads off one connection, and N video encodes on the machine also running the show. If your upload is comfortable, that is not a reason to put a server in the path.",
  },
  {
    who: "Restreamer",
    what: "Ranked by how often each was asked for on their own tracker, which is the honest ordering.",
    rows: [
      ["WebRTC / WHIP output", "Not in polyemesis. A sizeable subsystem; the real use case is sub-second self-monitoring."],
      ["Decklink / SDI capture, in and out", "Not in polyemesis. It needs an FFmpeg built with decklink, so a third image variant."],
      ["Multi-input compositing / video grid", "Not in polyemesis. Natural once multi-source is settled."],
      ["HDR 10-bit HEVC", "Partial in polyemesis. libx265 and hevc_nvenc exist; there is no HDR tone-map path."],
      ["LL-HLS", "Declined in polyemesis, not built. FFmpeg cannot emit LL-HLS partial segments at all, so the protocol would need a Go-side packager. Tuning instead took preview latency from 4.2–6.2 s to 2.2–3.2 s, measured."],
      ["Maturity", "Restreamer is established. polyemesis is at {release}."],
    ],
  },
  {
    who: "MistServer",
    what: "The one tool here that is a peer rather than a different shape, at a scale and protocol breadth this project does not approach.",
    rows: [
      ["Protocol breadth", "A directory of muxers, including WebRTC and its own SRT output. Here: SRT, SRTLA and RTMP in, RTMP/SRT out, HLS for preview."],
      ["Years of production deployment, and a commercial vendor behind it", "Here: {release}."],
      ["Selection without decoding", "A push target that only selects tracks is a pure copy end to end. Every polyemesis destination decodes and re-encodes its audio, always, because that is what mixing is."],
    ],
  },
  {
    who: "restream.io",
    what: "SaaS. Self-hosting wins on cost, privacy and limits by construction; restream.io wins wherever having somebody else run it is the feature.",
    rows: [
      ["Studio: browser production, remote guests", "Not in polyemesis. Multi-source plus compositing is the closest path."],
      ["Teams, roles, multiple workspaces", "Not in polyemesis, which has exactly one admin identity."],
      ["No server to run", "polyemesis is self-hosted by design."],
    ],
    tail: "Teams and roles is the one to take seriously. Access to the UI is full control of the server's streaming, so any multi-operator deployment (a church A/V team, an agency running several clients) hits that immediately.",
  },
];

/* The tools a reader is most likely to already have installed, and the one
 * distinction that holds against all of them.
 *
 * These get a section rather than three more table columns. Seven columns does
 * not survive a phone even inside overflow-x-auto, and the argument against a
 * plugin is not row-shaped anyway: it is a single sentence about select versus
 * mix, and it is worth more with the line of source that proves it attached.
 *
 * Every `src` below was read in that project's own repository on 2026-08-09.
 * Sourced from docs/COMPARISON.md, "They select a track. polyemesis mixes." */
/* `href` is optional and only two of the three have one: MistServer has no page
 * of its own yet. It is now covered by the facts file (§6, added 2026-08-15),
 * which is what it lacked — but a full page wants more than one section.
 *
 * `src` IS A QUOTATION OR IT IS ABSENT. The other two cards quote a real line
 * from the project's own repository, which is why they carry a file path and a
 * read date and why a reader can check them. MistServer's used to hold
 * "Util::wouldSelect() resolves audio= off the target's query string" — a
 * DESCRIPTION of what a function does, set in the same quoted-code treatment
 * that gives the other two their authority. It looked sourced and was not.
 *
 * So this card cites the facts file instead, and says so. Two kinds of evidence
 * on one page is fine; evidence that is dressed as a stronger kind than it is
 * is not. */
const selectors = [
  {
    href: "/vs/obs-multi-rtmp",
    name: "obs-multi-rtmp",
    what: "An OBS plugin. Each destination is another output from the stream PC, so N destinations is N uploads off one connection.",
    src: "struct AudioTrackConfig { int mixer_track; int output_track; };",
    file: "src/output-config.h",
    note: "Two integers: this mixer track goes in that output slot. No gain, no sum.",
  },
  {
    href: "/vs/aitum-multistream",
    name: "Aitum Multistream",
    what: "An OBS plugin. The destination's audio is picked in its output settings and stored as one number.",
    src: 'obs_audio_encoder_create(…, obs_data_get_int(settings, "audio_track"), …)',
    file: "multistream.cpp",
    note: "One mixer index per destination.",
  },
  {
    name: "MistServer",
    what: "A media server, and the one here that genuinely can pick tracks per push target: by track ID, codec, language, bitrate, or a first/last/optimal heuristic.",
    // No `src`/`file`: see the note above. This card's evidence is the facts
    // file, not a line of their source, and the page says which.
    source: "competitor-facts §6 · checked 15 Aug 2026",
    note: "It selects, and selects well. Its ROUTING SERVER has no amix, no loudnorm, no ebur128, but an external Stream Process (MistProcAV, an FFmpeg pipeline) can decode, filter and inject a track back into the buffer, so mixing is reachable, just not where the routing happens.",
  },
];
const cols = [
  { key: "polyemesis", name: "polyemesis", poly: true },
  { key: "restreamer", name: "Restreamer" },
  { key: "restream-io", name: "restream.io" },
];
const crumbs = [{ label: "polyemesis", href: "/" }, { label: "comparison" }];
const index = [
  { id: "glance", label: "Where they differ" },
  { id: "one", label: "Compared one at a time" },
  { id: "mixes", label: "They select a track" },
  { id: "shared", label: "What all three do" },
  { id: "other", label: "Choose another tool when" },
];
---

{/* The title used to lead with "polyemesis vs", which is a brand nobody is
    searching for yet, and spent its first eleven characters saying so. The
    category term is what has to win the click before the argument below can do
    any work — so the title states what the page IS, and the brand appears where
    it belongs, in the list of things being compared.

    The headline is untouched on purpose. An earlier audit proposed replacing
    "The audio is the difference." with a keyword string; it was rejected then
    and would be worse now that this page has four children carrying the
    long-tail terms. */}
<InnerPage
  title="Multistream tools compared: polyemesis vs the alternatives"
  description="Multistream to Twitch, YouTube and Kick from one ingest. Restreamer, restream.io, obs-multi-rtmp, Aitum, StreamElements and Streamlabs compared on audio."
  path="/comparison"
  crumbs={crumbs}
  heading="The audio is the difference."
  accent="audio"
  lede="polyemesis is built for one case, completely: this platform gets the clean mix, that one gets the full mix, from a single upload and a single video encode, with the picture never re-encoded to do it."
  index={index}
  band={{
    heading: "Still the right problem?",
    lede: "Then the next thing to read is the one that tells you what running it is actually like, and that is the quickstart rather than the download page.",
    secondary: { label: "This page, with its sources", href: "/docs/comparison" },
  }}
>
  <a slot="actions" class="btn btn-ghost" href="#glance">See the table</a>
  {releaseLine(release) && <Fragment slot="meta">Here: {releaseLine(release)}</Fragment>}

  <div class="cp">
    {/* THE PAGE SAYS "USE SOMETHING ELSE" BEFORE IT SAYS ANYTHING ELSE, and it
        is deliberately the second thing a reader meets rather than a footnote at
        the bottom. README.md and docs/COMPARISON.md both open on this sentence;
        a comparison page that carries the argument and drops the concession is
        not the same document with better styling, it is a less honest one.

        NOT the coral the rest of the page uses for audio. Coral is this site's
        "act here" accent, and this block exists to tell a particular reader not
        to. */}
    <div class="cp-lead">
      <p>
        That is the case the whole product is arranged around: the routing
        matrix, the per-destination loudness targets, the multitrack archive and
        the stems all exist because the audio is the hard part, and because
        everywhere else it is a track you pick rather than a mix you build.
      </p>
      <div class="panel cp-short">
        <p class="eyebrow">The short version</p>
        <p class="cp-short-main">
          If you do not need different audio per platform, Restreamer is a more
          mature product and you should probably use it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Multistreaming from the stream PC: obs-multi-rtmp is free, mature and
          enough. A server instead: Restreamer is more mature and far more widely
          deployed than this. No server at all: restream.io. An industrial-strength
          media server with real track selection: MistServer. polyemesis exists for
          the one case none of them serve. If that is not your problem, one of the
          tools above is the better fit.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section id="glance" class="cp-sec">
      <h2>Where they differ</h2>
      <p class="cp-intro">
        One axis, and it is the one this project exists for. The shared ground
        (everything all three do) is in the table below this one.
      </p>
      {/* The chooser here controls both tables: same column keys in both. */}
      <CompareTable
        caption="Where polyemesis, Restreamer and restream.io differ"
        firstHeader="Capability"
        cols={cols}
        rows={wins}
        chooser
        legend
      />
      {/* The second-audio-mix row's caveat, in the same convention the product
          uses: name the specific claim without evidence, and do not gate. */}
      <p class="cp-note">
        <span class="chip">experimental</span>
        <span>
          On the second-audio-mix row, and only where the destination is Twitch. The
          negotiation itself is proven: polyemesis’s tests reach ingest.twitch.tv on
          every run, and Twitch accepts a supported-GPU inventory, grants the VOD
          audio track and mints a key. What nobody has watched yet is a broadcast
          published through that key. Off Twitch the two-mix egress is a different
          mechanism and is not covered by this. Nothing is gated either way: a
          negotiation that does not succeed falls back to the ordinary Twitch ingest.
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="cp-note">
        The multitrack ask is not hypothetical. “Multiple audio tracks for Twitch”
        was an open request on{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/datarhei/restreamer/issues" rel="noopener">Restreamer's own tracker</a>{" "}
        when this was surveyed in July 2026: users asking it for the thing
        polyemesis was built to be.
      </p>
      <p class="cp-more"><a class="more" href="/#matrix">Try the routing matrix</a></p>
    </section>

    {/* HIGH ON THE PAGE, and that placement is the point.

        Most people arriving here typed a product name, not a category — they
        already have one of these installed and want to know what changes. Each
        page is linked with the argument it makes rather than a bare product
        name, because the arguments are genuinely different and a reader
        choosing between them should be able to tell which one is theirs. They
        are also the reason these are separate pages instead of more columns
        here: a Yes/No grid cannot say "both do this, and one costs more to
        change", which is the true shape of the plugin comparison. */}
    <section id="one" class="cp-sec">
      <h2>Compared one at a time</h2>
      <p class="cp-intro">
        If you already run something, start here. Each page compares polyemesis
        against one of them in full, with that tool's strengths stated and every
        claim dated against its own sources.
      </p>
      <div class="rows">
        {
          VS_INDEX.map((c) => (
            <a class="cp-vs" href={c.href}>
              <span class="cp-vs-name">{c.name}</span>
              <span class="cp-vs-kind">{c.kind}</span>
              <span class="cp-vs-line">{c.line}</span>
              <span class="more">Read the comparison</span>
            </a>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="cp-note">
        Twitch multistreaming is the case most of these are bought for, and all
        five do it. The tables below are what separates them once that is assumed.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* The tools a reader is most likely to already have installed, and the one
        distinction that holds against all of them.

        These get a section rather than more table columns: seven columns does
        not survive a phone, and the argument against a plugin is not row-shaped
        anyway. It is a single sentence about select versus mix, and it is worth
        more with the line of source that proves it attached. */}
    <section id="mixes" class="cp-sec">
      <h2>They select a track. polyemesis mixes.</h2>
      <p class="cp-intro">
        Most people weighing this up already have one of these installed. The
        distinction against every one of them is the same narrow one, and it is
        narrow enough to be worth checking rather than believing: they decide{" "}
        <em>which</em> track leaves for a destination. None of them decides what
        is in it.
      </p>
      <div class="cp-sel">
        {
          selectors.map((s) => (
            <div class="panel cp-card">
              <p class="cp-card-name">{s.name}</p>
              <p>{s.what}</p>
              {s.src && <CodeBlock code={s.src} variant="quote" />}
              {s.src && <p class="cp-src">{s.file} · read 2026-08-09</p>}
              {s.source && <p class="cp-src">{s.source}</p>}
              <p>{s.note}</p>
              {s.href && (
                <p>
                  <a class="more" href={s.href}>Full comparison against {s.name}</a>
                </p>
              )}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="cp-after">
        polyemesis sums instead: the tracks you chose, with a gain per
        input-channel-to-output-channel cell, and a loudness target measured after
        the routing rather than before it. That is a different operation, which is
        why per-destination audio is not a small patch to an OBS plugin.
      </p>
      <p class="cp-note">
        It costs something, and the cost belongs on this page too. Selecting a
        track is a copy; mixing means decoding the audio and encoding it again on
        every destination. Only the video is copied straight through. If one mix
        for everyone is fine, a plugin is free and you should use one.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="shared" class="cp-sec">
      <h2>What all three do</h2>
      <p class="cp-intro">
        The basic job is table stakes everywhere: ingest once, fan out, reconnect
        when a platform drops, show you whether it is working. Worth stating
        plainly: it means the choice between these three comes down to the
        audio, and not to whether the fundamentals are there.
      </p>
      <CompareTable
        caption="What polyemesis, Restreamer and restream.io all do"
        firstHeader="Shared ground"
        cols={cols}
        rows={shared}
      />
      <p class="cp-note">
        Two rows are not a tie and are left that way: restream.io needs no server
        from you at all, and Restreamer has a far longer public release history.
        Both are real advantages, and neither changes what happens to your audio.
      </p>
      {/* The hardware-encoding row's caveat. It is not in the cell because the
          cell would then be false in the other direction: all five ARE offered
          and all five are probed. Mirrors the footnote in docs/COMPARISON.md. */}
      <p class="cp-note">
        <span class="chip">experimental</span>
        <span>
          On the hardware-encoding row: all five encoders are offered, and each is
          probed with a real one-frame encode at startup so one that is listed but
          unusable is caught before a broadcast is. What is unverified is narrower:
          the command-line flags handed to NVENC, QSV, VA-API and AMF were read out
          of FFmpeg’s own option tables rather than measured on silicon.
          VideoToolbox is not in that set: a real encode with its own flags runs on
          every build.
        </span>
      </p>
      <p class="cp-note">
        The recording row said “Yes” for Restreamer until 9 August 2026 and was
        wrong. Recording is an open request on{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/datarhei/restreamer/issues/692" rel="noopener">their own tracker</a>,
        opened February 2024 and still open when it was checked. The datarhei
        Core underneath can write files; the Restreamer product does not put
        recording in front of you, which is why the request exists.
      </p>
      <p class="cp-note">
        Rows and cards carry their own check date where one exists. The oldest
        reads here are from July 2026, re-checked 9 August 2026; the MistServer,
        StreamElements and Streamlabs material was read 15–16 August 2026. These
        products move independently of this page, so check them before deciding on
        the strength of a row.
      </p>
    </section>

    {/* LIMITS STAY ON THE PAGE, in a lower section than the table.

        THE HEADING NAMES A CHOICE, NOT A DEFEAT (2026-11 design review). It was
        "Where polyemesis loses"; it is "Choose another tool when", and each
        block says when that tool is the better pick. The content is unchanged:
        the same rows, the same honesty, framed as advice to the reader. */}
    <section id="other" class="cp-sec">
      <h2>Choose another tool when</h2>
      <p class="cp-intro">
        Each of these is a job another tool is built for. They are worth reading
        before you spend an evening on an install: they follow from design
        decisions and the project's age, not from a roadmap, so the right tool
        for these jobs today is the one named.
      </p>
      <div class="rows">
        {
          losses.map((l) => (
            <div class="cp-loss">
              <h3>Choose {l.who} for</h3>
              <p class="cp-loss-what">{l.what}</p>
              <dl>
                {l.rows.map(([has, here]) => (
                  <div>
                    <dt>{has}</dt>
                    <dd>{fillRelease(here, release)}</dd>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </dl>
              {l.tail && <p class="cp-loss-tail">{l.tail}</p>}
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
      <p class="cp-note">
        These come from{" "}
        <a href="/docs/comparison">docs/COMPARISON.md</a>, which carries the same
        rows with their evidence, their issue numbers and the date each was
        checked, including the footnotes recording the rows that were wrong and
        were corrected, in both directions: some had gone stale understating this
        product, and twice a cell asserted a competitor lacked something they ship.
      </p>
      <p class="cp-more"><a class="more" href="/docs/quickstart">Read the quickstart</a></p>
    </section>
  </div>
</InnerPage>
